Horologist's Triumph
Ingredients: BLACK METAL + POWER METAL + EUPHORIC, ENERGETIC & JOLLY + Electric Piano
Creative Catalyst: its instruments are built from meteorite fragments and antique clockwork.

It sounds like a blizzard of shattered gears and runaway pendulum swings, where the frostbitten, shrieking tremolo of black metal is violently upended by the blinding, stadium-sized heroics of power metal. Beneath the high-speed blast beats and galloping twin-lead guitars runs an unapologetically jubilant, bouncing rhythm anchored by a dusty, over-driven Wurlitzer electric piano that plays jaunty ragtime-adjacent chords through a wall of blistering distortion. The vocals swing wildly between cavernous, black-metal corpse-shrieks and triumphant, multi-tracked power-metal choir anthems, creating an atmosphere that feels less like a gloomy graveyard and more like an ecstatic Victorian clockmaker discovering eternal motion while sliding down an ice-covered alp.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 185–200 BPM; a relentless, double-kick gallop that feels like a runaway Swiss train jumping the tracks with a smile.
- Harmonic Palette: Major-key pentatonic modal shifts layered over minor-key black metal tremolo, favoring bright, triumphant fifths that clash delightfully with dissonant chordal hammering.
- Production Signatures: The distinct clatter of antique escapement wheels bleeding into the microphone preamps; electric piano running through heavily overdriven tube amplifiers with spring reverb; ultra-compressed, razor-sharp blast beats mixed side-by-side with soaring, stadium-reverb choir stacks.
- Dynamics: Explosive and theatrical; tracks burst open with a frantic, joyous explosion of speed before dropping into a bouncing, syncopated electric piano groove, only to catapult upward into towering, tear-jerking harmonic resolutions.

Scene & Origin Lore
In the frost-laced watchmaking valleys of the Jura Mountains in 2018, a cabal of disgruntled horologists and underground metal archivists began melting down iron-rich chondrite meteorites to forge custom guitars and gear chassis driven by authentic 18th-century anchor escapements. Rejecting the bleak misanthropy of traditional black metal and the sterile cheese of modern power, this subculture adopted an aesthetic of immaculate top hats paired with studded leather corsets, brass-rimmed goggles, and soot-stained waistcoats. Their rituals—known as "Tick-Tock Sabbaths"—involve gathering in abandoned clocktowers at dawn to blast high-speed, joyous anthems while manually winding hundreds of mechanical pocket watches in unison, celebrating the sheer, unbridled ecstasy of cheating time through sheer rhythmic velocity.

Typical Instruments: Meteorite-cast electric guitars with internal spring-chimes, antique clockwork double-bass pedals, distorted Wurlitzer electric piano, brass bell-tree percussion, and throat-shredding choir ensembles.

